{"step_index":226,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:39:33Z","thinking":"**Determining the maximum graph radius**\n\nThe goal is to determine if the radius of a graph, G, is always less than or equal to the floor of the number of vertices divided by 2. The maximum radius is achieved by a path graph, $P_n$. The radius of this path graph is the floor of n/2.\nSince the path graph provides the maximum possible radius for any connected graph with the same number of vertices, all other graphs will have a radius less than or equal to this value.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Dominating Set Constraints**\n\nThe radius of any connected graph G with n vertices is less than or equal to floor(n/2).  Now analyzing the total dominating set S. If |S| >= floor(n/2), then rad(G) <= floor(n/2) <= |S|.\n\nIf |S| = 2 or 3, it is proven that rad(G) <= |S|. The concern is when 4 <= |S| < floor(n/2), considering cases like a star graph with the dominating set being the center and leaves. The diameter will be small in this type of graph.\n\nConsider the path graph where |S| >= n/2. The radius, in general, will be small relative to n.\n\n\n**Formulating Proof Strategy**\n\nThe goal is to prove `GraphCalc.radius G ≤ GraphCalc.totalDominationNumber G`, where the domination number relates to |S|. Two extremes are considered: path graphs and graphs with high-degree vertices.\n\nThe path graph extreme provides a maximum radius of $\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor \\le |S|$ and the high-degree vertex case has radius no more than $\\lfloor |S|/2 \\rfloor + 1$. Both extremes and graphs in between satisfy $\\text{rad}(G) \\le |S|$.\n\nThe Lean 4 theorem to prove is `prove_2a2555cc`. The rules forbid axioms. A previously proven lemma `ballWithin_univ_of_subset G v 1 S hdom hsub` may be useful.\n\n\n**Defining the Optimal Vertex**\n\nConsidering the case where no vertex $v$ exists such that the ball of radius 2 centered at $v$ contains the set $S$. If no such vertex exists, then $|S|$ is at least 4. The objective is to construct a vertex $v$ such that the ball of radius $|S| - 1$ centered at $v$ contains $S$.\n\nDefining the function `maxDistToS` to find the maximum distance from a vertex to the set S. Given that the vertex set is a `Fintype` and nonempty, this function has a minimum value. Choosing the vertex $x_0$ that minimizes `maxDistToS`, denoted as $D_0$, to be the maximum distance from $x_0$ to any element in $S$.\n\n**Deriving the Radius Bound**\n\nThe goal is to show $D_0 \\le |S| - 1$. Assuming $D_0 \\le |S| - 1$, then for every element $s$ of $S$, $dist(x_0, s) \\le |S| - 1$, implying $S$ is a subset of the ball of radius $|S| - 1$ centered at $x_0$. This would further imply that the ball of radius $|S|$ centered at $x_0$ covers the entire graph. Consequently, the graph's radius is at most $|S|$.\n\nThe central question now is: why is $D_0 \\le |S| - 1$? The goal is to obtain a contradiction by assuming $D_0 \\ge |S|$. If $D_0 \\ge |S|$, it means that for any vertex, there is a point in S with a high distance. This case needs to be proven impossible in Lean.\n\n\n**Refining the Lower Bound Analysis**\n\nAssuming the existence of a vertex $x$ such that the distance to every vertex in $S$ is at least $|S|$. This creates a shortest path $y_0$ to $y_L$, where $L \\ge |S|$. Each $y_i$ has a neighbor in $S$. The Pigeonhole Principle implies that there must exist $i < j$ such that $y_i$ and $y_j$ share a neighbor in $S$, leading to dist($y_i$, $y_j$) <= 2, but $j - i$ >= |S|.\n\nThis shortest path can be used to construct a walk of length 2 from $y_i$ to $y_j$, so the distance $j - i$ should be at most 2. Consider every 3rd node.\n\n\n**Bounding the Graph's Diameter**\n\nA path $P$ is considered with vertices $y_0$ to $y_L$. Constructing a function, f, mapping an index to a neighbor in S.  The condition $f(i) = f(j)$ implies dist($y_i$, $y_j$) <= 2. Since $P$ is shortest, $f(i) = f(j)$ then $|i-j| <= 2$.\n\nThe contrapositive indicates that if $|i - j| \\ge 3$, then $f(i) \\ne f(j)$.  An index set $I$ can then be constructed containing indices from the path with a distance of at least 3.  The map from $I$ to $S$ is injective, implying $|I| \\le |S|$.\n\nIf the path length L >= 3|S|, then a contradiction arises from the injective map and the Pigeonhole Principle, forcing an upper bound on the path's length, thus the graph's diameter. The distance between any two vertices is at most $3|S| - 1$.\n\n\n**Evaluating Subagent Utilization**\n\nThe task explicitly directs toward proof construction. Considering the available subagents, the most relevant options are the `teamwork_preview_conductor` and `teamwork_preview_orchestrator`.\n\nThe user instructions identify the agent's role as the Pipeline Conductor, which suggests that coordinating subagent execution is a primary function. A key is the call to `invoke_subagent`.\n\nBased on the prompt, it is reasonable to begin by investigating how to leverage the available subagents to construct the formal proof.\n\n\n**Evaluating Direct Proof Feasibility**\n\nConsidering the task's instructions and available resources, it is necessary to assess if direct proof construction is feasible, or if subagent utilization is essential. The agent's role as \"Pipeline Conductor\" suggests subagent invocation, but the direct access to tools also allows for standalone operation.\n\nThe prompt indicates a preference for direct operation unless specific subagent expertise is required, so the proof itself will be attempted without initially spawning other agents. Given this framework, the proof will first be attempted directly.\n\n\n**Refining Proof Construction**\n\nThe overall goal is to show the radius is bounded by the total domination number. Using existing lemmas, consider `radius_le_eccentricity` and `eccentricity_le_of_ballWithin_eq_univ`. The core challenge lies in showing that for some vertex, its eccentricity is less than or equal to the total domination number, $|S|$.\n\nThe strategy is to find a vertex $v$ and a value $k$, potentially $|S| - 1$ such that `GraphCalc.ballWithin G k v = Finset.univ`, implying the eccentricity of $v$ is at most $k$. To achieve that it is necessary to construct the existence of a vertex within a certain ball.\n\nThe statement `∃ v : V, S ⊆ GraphCalc.ballWithin G (S.card - 1) v` requires proof, given that `S` is a total dominating set. The existing result when `S.card = 2` is known.\n\n\n**Analyzing Larger Cardinality Cases**\n\nConsidering the case where $|S| \\ge 4$. If $|S|=3$, we know $S \\subseteq \\text{ballWithin G 2 v}$.\n\nA sequence of vertices in $S$ is defined: $s_1$ and its neighbor $s_2$. If another vertex, $s_3$, isn't adjacent to $s_1$ or $s_2$, it forms a separate component. Then, $s_3$ has its own neighbor, $s_4$.\n\nA path of length at most 3 connects these components. The path's midpoint has distance at most 2 to all 4 vertices.  So for 2 components of size 2, $S \\subseteq \\text{ballWithin G 3 v}$.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"grep_search","args":{"MatchPerLine":true,"Query":"dist_","SearchPath":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/lean_project/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Combinatorics/SimpleGraph/Metric.lean","toolAction":"Searching the web","toolSummary":"Grep dist_ in Metric.lean"}}]}
